    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we first need to understand the independence of the events. The probability of two independent events both occurring is the product of their individual probabilities. \n\nThe probability of rain on Saturday is given as 60%, which can be expressed as a decimal:\n\\[ P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) = 0.60 \\]\n\nSimilarly, the probability of rain on Sunday is given as 25%, which can be expressed as a decimal:\n\\[ P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.25 \\]\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is the product of the individual probabilities:\n\\[ P(\\text{Rain on both days}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15 \\]\n\nTo convert this decimal probability to a percentage, we multiply by 100:\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is:\n\\[ \\boxed{15} \\]",
