    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the fact that the probability of two independent events occurring is the product of their individual probabilities. Here, the probability it will rain on Saturday is 60% (or 0.60), and the probability it will rain on Sunday is 25% (or 0.25).\n\nLet's denote the probability of rain on Saturday as \\( P(S) = 0.60 \\) and the probability of rain on Sunday as \\( P(S^c) = 1 - P(S) = 1 - 0.60 = 0.40 \\), where \\( S^c \\) represents the event that it does not rain on Saturday. Similarly, the probability of rain on Sunday is \\( P(S^c) = 0.25 \\).\n\nSince the rain events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days, \\( P(S \\cap S^c) \\), is the product of the probabilities of rain on Saturday, \\( P(S) \\), and the probability of no rain on Sunday, \\( P(S^c) \\):\n\n\\[ P(S \\cap S^c) = P(S) \\times P(S^c) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 \\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[ 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15 \\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\% \\]\n\nThus, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
